{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1793891642,"nonce":"3llE2cDX6f5VAbgeFh8DpJG+OFw=","message":"Implemented allocation-free operand traversal in scalar replacement using existing HIR instruction and terminator visitors. Phi edges remain explicit; indirect callee uses remain covered. Candidate rules, invalidation and optimizer rounds are unchanged.\n\nAllocation audit (pass only, 1000 arithmetic instructions and one scalarized malloc): 2035 allocations / 313172 requested bytes before, 31 allocations / 281108 bytes after. The one-instruction control falls from 29 to 23 allocations. The regression test fails on the baseline and passes on the candidate; an analysis-only indirect-callee escape test preserves the backing allocation.\n\nValidation: 35 focused compiler checks passed, including allocation audits, scalar replacement, heap scalarization and partial escape. Lua 46 conformance groups plus foreign/native-snapshot tests and Python 50 groups plus foreign tests passed. TS local execution: 1413 passes. Complete pinned official corpus: 5948 files, 6825 cases; PASS 1151, FAIL 12, UNSUPPORTED 2234, SKIP 3428; zero regressions and identical per-case statuses/options and corpus metadata against the previous validated run. Existing failures/unsupported/skips remain separate; baseline unchanged. All-target clippy with LLVM/allocation-audit and formatting/diff checks passed.\n\nNUC: frozen binaries, native snapshots enabled, LLVM disabled, alternating pairs, temperature/load gates, no Cargo/rustc during timing. Nine workloads x 21 pairs = 378 clean samples with reference checks and no throttle increments. Wall time is mostly unchanged: method_call 28.497 -\u003e 27.710 ms, binary_trees 146.595 -\u003e 145.990, records 82.567 -\u003e 82.374, pipeline 321.811 -\u003e 319.296, tokenize 106.471 -\u003e 105.995, collatz 143.072 -\u003e 143.091, method_short 19.364 -\u003e 19.147, method_long 45.742 -\u003e 45.792. These do not establish a broad speedup.\n\nOperator-overload initially showed 32.322 -\u003e 32.613 ms (paired median ratio 1.01293, exploratory bootstrap 95% interval 1.00205..1.02969). A separate fresh 31-pair control did not reproduce it: 32.288 -\u003e 32.169 ms, paired ratio 0.98627, interval 0.97199..1.02992. All 62 control samples passed output/exit/throttle checks. No consistent regression established; no speedup claimed.\n\nSeparate phase diagnostics (OSR/snapshots disabled symmetrically) show identical inventories and normalized contents for all 17 captured optimized HIR bodies and identical optimizer progress. Main-function scalar replacement medians: method_call 0.39 -\u003e 0.37 ms, op_overload 0.32 -\u003e 0.30, records 0.42 -\u003e 0.40, tokenize 0.29 -\u003e 0.27. These are instrumented pass costs, not ordinary wall time.\n\nEvidence: /private/tmp/zyntax-sra-operands contains source/binary manifests, failing baseline audit, passing validation logs, corpus comparison, raw samples and analysis. NUC evidence archive: /private/tmp/zyntax-sra-operands/zyntax-sra-operands-evidence.tar.gz, SHA256 45bb6a6e5158b9ad695c4d36cc8ae898d382c7817f1e5f4f1e22dda2bfb07c28. Remote copies: /tmp/zyntax-sra-operands on nuc. The NUC reservation is released. Parent 1be184c remains open for larger HIR preparation costs.","files":null}]}